  "captions": [
    {"start": 2.4, "end": 14.48, "en": "In December 1955, on a bus in Montgomery, Alabama, a Black woman named Rosa Parks was ordered to give her seat to a white passenger.", "ja": ""},
    {"start": 14.706, "end": 15.056, "en": "No.", "ja": ""},
    {"start": 14.933, "end": 18.1, "en": "How short, how quiet those words were!", "ja": ""},
    {"start": 19.22, "end": 23.8, "en": "Yet her single word changed the course of American history.", "ja": ""},
    {"start": 24.74, "end": 26.94, "en": "She was arrested on the spot.", "ja": ""},
    {"start": 28.58, "end": 35.92, "en": "In those days, the law separated the races, and Black people could only sit in certain seats.", "ja": ""},
    {"start": 35.92, "end": 44.04, "en": "The law, which had made discrimination a system, had long cast a shadow on society.", "ja": ""},
    {"start": 44.04, "end": 49.56, "en": "Parks' refusal sparked the Montgomery Bus Boycott.", "ja": ""},
    {"start": 51.92, "end": 58.66, "en": "Reverend Martin Luther King led the way, and the Black citizens of the town stopped riding the buses.", "ja": ""},
    {"start": 60.02, "end": 62.4, "en": "What a strong protest it was!", "ja": ""},
    {"start": 63.82, "end": 74.18, "en": "The following year, a federal court ruled bus segregation unconstitutional, and the U.S. Supreme Court upheld that decision.", "ja": ""},
    {"start": 76.32, "end": 80.92, "en": "The No of a single citizen helped change society.", "ja": ""},
    {"start": 80.92, "end": 87.18, "en": "Let us never forget such a single word, filled with courage.", "ja": ""}
  ]
